Reconstructing tree for MEF2(Myocyte-specific enhancer factor 2) gene family across chordates. 
Known to have at least 4 paralogs, MEF2A, MEF2B, MEF2C, MEF2D. 
The family is well conserved, with even yeast having a homolog. 



The family has many isoforms, which makes it hard to build protein trees and translation alignments. 
The longest isoform was used to retain all possible coding regions, and only the common region between homologs would be used. 
Homologs would be searched through BLASTP, with known species that reflect the diversity of chordata.(and outgroups!) 






** The project was canceled due to: 
Too little information is presumed to exist to infer gene history. 
Conserved regions are too conserved, while unconserved regions are too unconserved. 
Using nucleotides of coding regions for translation alignment would not also be feasible as those regions would change even faster than the exon regions.  

Also, BLAST db nomenclature is not to be trusted. 
Genes are named in conventions that does not integrate phylogenetic information. 
(They at least seems to reflect sequence similarity, but who knows?)




(I spent almost 3 hours searching homologs in BLASTP for nothing... but learning is costly... )